          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Define the function ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "f(x)=\\dfrac{x-a}{x-b}",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", where ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "x\\ne b",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ".",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p40-q73a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find $f'(x)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f'(x)=\\dfrac{a-b}{(x-b)^2}$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f'(x)&=\\dfrac{(x-b)(1)-(x-a)(1)}{(x-b)^2}\\\\f'(x)&=\\dfrac{a-b}{(x-b)^2}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p40-q73b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that if $a>b$, then $f'(x)>0$. What is the geometric significance of this?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Since $a-b>0$ and $(x-b)^2>0$ for $x\\ne b$, $f'(x)>0$. The gradients of the tangents are always positive.",
          "worked": "For $x\\ne b$, $(x-b)^2>0$. If $a>b$, then $a-b>0$, so $f'(x)=\\dfrac{a-b}{(x-b)^2}>0$. Every tangent to the curve has positive gradient.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p40-q73c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Show that if $a<b$, then $f'(x)<0$. What is the geometric significance of this?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Since $a-b<0$ and $(x-b)^2>0$ for $x\\ne b$, $f'(x)<0$. The gradients of the tangents are always negative.",
          "worked": "For $x\\ne b$, $(x-b)^2>0$. If $a<b$, then $a-b<0$, so $f'(x)=\\dfrac{a-b}{(x-b)^2}<0$. Every tangent to the curve has negative gradient.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p40-q73d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Show that $f'(x)=0$ when $a=b$. Was this result expected?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Yes. When $a=b$, $f(x)=1$ for $x\\ne b$, so $f'(x)=0$.",
          "worked": "When $a=b$, the numerator of $f'(x)=\\dfrac{a-b}{(x-b)^2}$ is zero, so $f'(x)=0$ for $x\\ne b$. This is expected because $f(x)=\\dfrac{x-b}{x-b}=1$ throughout its domain.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      }
    ]
  },
